+### Moving definitions between installations
+
+An export carries no database ids, so it can be imported into another installation (from a test
+system into production, for example):
+
+```json
+{"name":"Expenses","version":3,"steps":[
+  {"order":1,"kind":"SINGLE","assignee":"tomas@example.com","group":null,"quorum":0,"sla":"PT8H"},
+  {"order":2,"kind":"QUORUM","assignee":null,"group":"finance","quorum":2,"sla":"PT1H30M"}]}
+```
+
+- A `SINGLE` step names its approver by email address and a group step names its group; the import
+  finds them on the installation it runs on. An unknown group, or an address no account has, fails
+  the import with 400 and nothing is created. Steps keep their order and kind.
+- `sla` is an ISO-8601 duration in whole minutes, from `PT1M` to `PT720H` (`P1D` is read as
+  `PT24H`). An export writes hours and minutes, such as `PT8H` or `PT1H30M`.
+- The import always creates a draft, to be reviewed and published as usual. Without `version` it
+  takes the next version of its name. With `version`, that number is kept only when it is above
+  every version of the name on this installation; otherwise the import is refused with 409, so an
+  import never reuses an existing version number or goes below the latest one. Remove `version` to
+  import a definition again on the installation it came from.
+- Exporting the imported draft gives the same steps again.
+
 ## Operator rules
 
 - A draft is the only definition that can change; a published one is refused with 409 when steps are added,
